    # u2(0,pi) is equivalent to X gate (up to global phase)
    # u3(pi,0,pi) = X gate (up to global phase)
    # So let's interpret the gates:
    # q[0]: u2(0,pi) twice -> X * X = I (identity)
    # q[1]: u2(0,pi), then after cx q[1],q[3], another u2(0,pi)
    # q[2]: u2(0,pi), then after cx q[2],q[3], another u2(0,pi)
    # q[3]: u3(pi,0,pi) then u2(0,pi) -> X * X = I (identity)
    # So q[3] starts as |0>, then X, then X again = |0>
    # The two CX gates are controlled by q[1] and q[2] targeting q[3].
    # The u2(0,pi) on q[1] and q[2] before and after CX are X gates.
    # So q[1] and q[2] are flipped before and after CX.
    # Let's rewrite the logic:
    # q[1]: X -> CX(q[1],q[3]) -> X
    # q[2]: X -> CX(q[2],q[3]) -> X
    # q[0]: X -> X (identity)
    # q[3]: X -> X (identity)
    # The effect of X-CX-X on control qubit is to flip it before and after CX, effectively toggling the control.
    # This is equivalent to CX with inverted control qubit.
    # So the circuit applies two inverted-control CX gates on q[3] controlled by q[1] and q[2].
    # This is a pattern used in the Bernstein-Vazirani algorithm oracle implementation.
    # The initial X gates on q[1] and q[2] prepare the input in |1> state, then the CX flips q[3] if control is 0.
    # The q[0] qubit is untouched (identity).
    # This matches the Bernstein-Vazirani oracle for a hidden bitstring 110 (q[1]=1, q[2]=1, q[0]=0).
    # So the circuit implements the Bernstein-Vazirani oracle for bitstring '110'.
    # The expected output on all-zero input is the oracle marking the hidden bitstring.

